|  * [340]Document markup languages
|~ L340:4-34
|  * [341]Comparison of browser engines
|~ L341:4-38
|  * [342]v
|~ L342:4-10
|  * [343]t
|~ L343:4-10
|  * [344]e
|~ L344:4-10
|
|Hypertext Markup Language (HTML) is the standard [345]markup language[346][a]
|~ B:0-25 B:27-31 L345:49-69 L346:69-77
|for documents designed to be displayed in a [347]web browser. It defines the
|~ L347:44-60
|content and structure of [348]web content. It is often assisted by technologies
|~ L348:25-41
|such as [349]Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) and [350]scripting languages such as
|~ L349:8-35 L350:46-70
|[351]JavaScript.
|~ L351:0-15
|
|[352]Web browsers receive HTML documents from a [353]web server or from local
|~ L352:0-17 L353:48-63
|storage and [354]render the documents into [355]multimedia web pages. HTML
|~ L354:12-23 L355:43-58
|describes the structure of a [356]web page [357]semantically and originally
|~ L356:29-42 L357:43-60
|included cues for its appearance.
|
|[358]HTML elements are the building blocks of HTML pages. With HTML constructs,
|~ L358:0-18
|[359]images and other objects such as [360]interactive forms may be embedded
|~ L359:0-11 L360:38-60
|into the rendered page. HTML provides a means to create [361]structured
|~ L361:56-71
|documents by denoting structural [362]semantics for text such as headings,
|~ L361:0-9 L362:33-47
|paragraphs, lists, [363]links, quotes, and other items. HTML elements are
|~ L363:19-29
|delineated by tags, written using [364]angle brackets. Tags such as <img> and
|~ U:14-18 L364:34-53
|<input> directly introduce content into the page. Other tags such as <p> and its
|corresponding closing tag </p> surround and provide information about document
|text and may include sub-element tags. [365]Browsers do not display the HTML
|~ L365:39-52
|tags, but use them to interpret the content of the page.
|
|HTML can embed programs written in a [366]scripting language such as
|~ L366:37-60
|[367]JavaScript, which affects the behavior and content of web pages. The
|~ L367:0-15
|inclusion of CSS defines the look and layout of content. The [368]World Wide Web
|~ L368:61-80
|Consortium (W3C), former maintainer of the HTML and current maintainer of the
|~ L368:0-10
|CSS standards, has encouraged the use of [369]CSS over explicit presentational
|~ L369:41-49
|HTML since 1997.[370][update][371][3]
|~ L370:16-29 L371:29-37
|
|History
|~ B:0-7
|
|Development
|~ B:0-11
|
|[372][Photograph of Tim Berners-Lee in April 2009]
|~ L372:0-50
|[373]Tim Berners-Lee in April 2009
|~ L373:0-20
|
|In 1980, the [374]physicist [375]Tim Berners-Lee, a contractor at [376]CERN,
|~ L374:13-27 L375:28-48 L376:66-75
|proposed and prototyped [377]ENQUIRE, a system for CERN researchers to use and
|~ L377:24-36
|share documents. In 1989, Berners-Lee wrote a memo proposing an
|[378]Internet-based [379]hypertext system.[380][4] Berners-Lee specified HTML
|~ L378:0-13 L379:20-34 L380:42-50
|and wrote the browser and server software in late 1990. That year, Berners-Lee
|and CERN [381]data systems engineer [382]Robert Cailliau collaborated on a joint
|~ L381:9-26 L382:36-56
|request for funding, but the project was not formally adopted by CERN. In his
|personal notes of 1990, Berners-Lee listed "some of the many areas in which
|hypertext is used"; an [383]encyclopedia is the first entry.[384][5]
|~ L383:23-40 L384:60-68
|
|The first publicly available description of HTML was a document called "HTML
|Tags",[385][6] first mentioned on the Internet by Tim Berners-Lee in late
|~ L385:6-14
|1991.[386][7][387][8] It describes 18 elements comprising the initial,
|~ L386:5-13 L387:13-21
|relatively simple design of HTML. Except for the hyperlink tag, these were
|strongly influenced by [388]CERN SGML, an in-house [389]Standard Generalized
|~ L388:23-37 L389:51-76
|Markup Language (SGML)-based documentation format at CERN. Eleven of these
|~ L389:0-15
|elements still exist in HTML 4.[390][9]
|~ L390:31-39
|
|HTML is a [391]markup language that [392]web browsers use to interpret and
|~ L391:10-30 L392:36-53
|[393]compose text, images, and other material into visible or audible web pages.
|~ L393:0-12
|Default characteristics for every item of HTML markup are defined in the
|browser, and these characteristics can be altered or enhanced by the web page
|designer's additional use of [394]CSS. Many of the text elements are mentioned
|~ L394:29-37
|in the 1988 ISO technical report TR 9537 Techniques for using SGML, which
|~ U:41-66
|describes the features of early text formatting languages such as that used by
|the [395]RUNOFF command developed in the early 1960s for the [396]CTSS
|~ L395:4-23 L396:61-70
|(Compatible Time-Sharing System) operating system. These formatting commands
|were derived from the commands used by typesetters to manually format documents.
|However, the SGML concept of generalized markup is based on elements (nested
|annotated ranges with attributes) rather than merely print effects, with
|separate structure and markup. HTML has been progressively moved in this
|direction with CSS.
|
|Berners-Lee considered HTML to be an application of SGML. It was formally
|defined as such by the [397]Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) with the
|~ L397:23-59
|mid-1993 publication of the first proposal for an HTML specification, the
|"Hypertext Markup Language (HTML)" [398]Internet Draft by Berners-Lee and
|~ L398:35-54
|[399]Dan Connolly, which included an SGML [400]document type definition to
|~ L399:0-17 L400:42-71
|define the syntax.[401][10][402][11] The draft expired after six months, but was
|~ L401:18-27 L402:27-36
|notable for its acknowledgment of the [403]NCSA Mosaic browser's custom tag for
|~ L403:38-54
|embedding in-line images, reflecting the IETF's philosophy of basing standards
|on successful prototypes. Similarly, [404]Dave Raggett's competing Internet
|~ L404:37-54
|Draft, "HTML+ (Hypertext Markup Format)", from late 1993, suggested
|standardizing already-implemented features like tables and fill-out
|forms.[405][12]
|~ L405:6-15
|
|After the HTML and HTML+ drafts expired in early 1994, the IETF created an HTML
|Working Group. In 1995, this working group completed "HTML 2.0", the first HTML
|specification intended to be treated as a standard against which future
|implementations should be based.[406][13]
|~ L406:32-41
|
|Further development under the auspices of the IETF was stalled by competing
|interests. Since 1996,[407][update] the HTML specifications have been
|~ L407:22-35
|maintained, with input from commercial software vendors, by the [408]World Wide
|~ L408:64-79
|Web Consortium (W3C).[409][14] In 2000, HTML became an international standard
|~ L408:0-14 L409:21-30
|([410]ISO/[411]IEC 15445:2000). HTML 4.01 was published in late 1999, with
|~ L410:1-9 L411:10-18
|further errata published through 2001. In 2004, development began on HTML5 in
|the [412]Web Hypertext Application Technology Working Group (WHATWG), which
|~ L412:4-59
|became a joint deliverable with the W3C in 2008, and was completed and
|standardized on 28 October 2014.[413][15]
|~ L413:32-41
|
|HTML version timeline
|~ B:0-21
|
|This section is in [414]list format but may read better as [415]prose. You can
|~ B:13-19 BL414:19-28 B:28-59 BL415:59-69
|help by [416]converting this section, if appropriate. [417]Editing help is
|~ L416:8-36 L417:54-71
|available. (November 2025)
|~ U:11-26
|
|HTML 2
|~ B:0-6
|
|24 November 1995
|    HTML 2.0 was published as [418]RFC [419]1866. Supplemental [420]RFCs added
|~ L418:30-38 L419:39-48 L420:63-72
|    capabilities:
|      * 25 November 1995: [421]RFC [422]1867 (form-based file upload)
|~ L421:26-34 L422:35-44
|      * May 1996: [423]RFC [424]1942 (tables)
|~ L423:18-26 L424:27-36
|      * August 1996: [425]RFC [426]1980 (client-side image maps)
|~ L425:21-29 L426:30-39
|      * January 1997: [427]RFC [428]2070 ([429]internationalization)
|~ L427:22-30 L428:31-40 L429:42-67
|
|HTML 3
|~ B:0-6
|
|14 January 1997
|    HTML 3.2[430][16] was published as a [431]W3C Recommendation. It was the
|~ L430:12-21 L431:41-64
|    first version developed and standardized exclusively by the W3C, as the IETF
|    had closed its HTML Working Group on 12 September 1996.[432][17]
|~ L432:59-68
|    Initially code-named "Wilbur",[433][18] HTML 3.2 dropped math formulas
|~ L433:34-43
|    entirely, reconciled overlap among various proprietary extensions and
|    adopted most of [434]Netscape's visual markup tags. Netscape's [435]blink
|~ L434:20-33 L435:67-77
|    element and [436]Microsoft's [437]marquee element were omitted due to a
|~ L435:4-11 L436:16-30 L437:33-53
|    mutual agreement between the two companies.[438][14] A markup for
|~ L438:47-56
|    mathematical formulas similar to that of HTML was standardized 14 months
|    later in [439]MathML.
|~ L439:13-24
|
|HTML 4
|~ B:0-6
|
|18 December 1997
|    HTML 4.0[440][19] was published as a W3C Recommendation. It offers three
|~ L440:12-21
|    variations:
|      * Strict, in which deprecated elements are forbidden
|      * Transitional, in which deprecated elements are allowed
|      * Frameset, in which mostly only [441]frame related elements are allowed.
|~ L441:39-49
|    Initially code-named "Cougar",[442][18] HTML 4.0 adopted many
|~ L442:34-43
|    browser-specific element types and attributes, but also sought to phase out
|    Netscape's visual markup features by marking them as [443]deprecated in
|~ L443:57-72
|    favor of style sheets. HTML 4 is an SGML application conforming to ISO
|    8879 - SGML.[444][20]
|~ L444:16-25
|24 April 1998
|    HTML 4.0[445][21] was reissued with minor edits without incrementing the
|~ L445:12-21
|    version number.
|24 December 1999
|    HTML 4.01[446][22] was published as a W3C Recommendation. It offers the same
|~ L446:13-22
|    three variations as HTML 4.0 and its last errata[447][23] were published on
|~ L447:52-61
|    12 May 2001.
|May 2000
|    ISO/IEC 15445:2000[448][24] ("[449]ISO HTML", based on HTML 4.01 Strict) was
|~ L448:22-31 L449:34-42
|    published as an ISO/IEC international standard.[450][25] In the ISO, this
|~ L450:51-60
|    standard is in the domain of the [451]ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 34 (ISO/IEC Joint
|~ L451:37-61
|    Technical Committee 1, Subcommittee 34 - Document description and processing
|    languages).[452][24]
|~ L452:15-24
|        After HTML 4.01, there were no new versions of HTML for many years, as
|        the development of the parallel, XML-based language XHTML occupied the
|        W3C's HTML Working Group.
|
|HTML 5
|~ B:0-6
|
|Main article: [453]HTML5
|~ L453:14-24
|28 October 2014
|    HTML5[454][26] was published as a W3C Recommendation.[455][27]
|~ L454:9-18 L455:57-66
|1 November 2016
|    HTML 5.1[456][28] was published as a W3C Recommendation.[457][29][458][30]
|~ L456:12-21 L457:60-69 L458:69-78
|14 December 2017
|    HTML 5.2[459][31] was published as a W3C Recommendation.[460][32][461][33]
|~ L459:12-21 L460:60-69 L461:69-78
|
|HTML draft version timeline
|~ B:0-27
|
|October 1991
|    HTML Tags,[462][7] an informal CERN document listing 18 HTML tags, was first
|~ U:4-13 L462:14-22
|    mentioned in public.
|June 1992
|    First informal draft of the HTML DTD,[463][34] with seven subsequent
|~ L463:41-50
|    revisions (15 July, 6 August, 18 August, 17 November, 18 November, 20
|    November, and 22 November)[464][35][465][36][466][37]
|~ L464:30-39 L465:39-48 L466:48-57
|November 1992
|    HTML DTD 1.1 (the first with a version number, based on RCS revisions, which
|    start with 1.1 rather than 1.0), an informal draft[467][37]
|~ L467:54-63
|June 1993
|    Hypertext Markup Language[468][38] was published by the [469]IETF IIIR
|~ L468:29-38 L469:60-69
|    Working Group as an Internet Draft (a rough proposal for a standard). It was
|    replaced by a second version[470][39] one month later.
|~ L470:32-41
|November 1993
|    HTML+ was published by the IETF as an Internet Draft and was a competing
|    proposal to the Hypertext Markup Language draft. It expired in July
|    1994.[471][40]
|~ L471:9-18
|November 1994
|    First draft (revision 00) of HTML 2.0 published by the IETF[472][41] (called
|~ L472:63-72
|    "HTML 2.0" starting with revision 02[473][42]), that finally led to the
|~ L473:40-49
|    publication of [474]RFC [475]1866 in November 1995.[476][43]
|~ L474:19-27 L475:28-37 L476:55-64
|April 1995 (authored March 1995)
|    HTML 3.0[477][44] was proposed as a standard to the IETF, but the proposal
|~ L477:12-21
|    expired five months later (28 September 1995)[478][45] without further
|~ L478:49-58
|    action. It included many of the capabilities that were in Raggett's HTML+
|    proposal, such as support for tables, text flow around figures, and the
|    display of complex mathematical formulas.[479][45]
|~ L479:45-54
|    W3C began development of its own [480]Arena browser as a [481]test bed for
|~ L480:37-55 L481:61-74
|    HTML 3 and Cascading Style Sheets,[482][46][483][47][484][48] but HTML 3.0
|~ L482:38-47 L483:47-56 L484:56-65
|    did not succeed for several reasons. The draft was considered very large at
|    150 pages and the pace of browser development, as well as the number of
|    interested parties, had outstripped the resources of the IETF.[485][14]
|~ L485:66-75
|    Browser vendors, including Microsoft and Netscape at the time, chose to
|    implement different subsets of HTML 3's draft features as well as to
|    introduce their own extensions to it.[486][14] (See [487]browser wars.)
|~ L486:41-50 L487:56-73
|    These included extensions to control stylistic aspects of documents,
|    contrary to the "belief [of the academic engineering community] that such
|    things as text color, background texture, font size, and font face were
|    definitely outside the scope of a language when their only intent was to
|    specify how a document would be organized."[488][14] Dave Raggett, who has
|~ L488:47-56
|    been a W3C Fellow for many years, has commented for example: "To a certain
|    extent, Microsoft built its business on the Web by extending HTML
|    features."[489][14]
|~ L489:14-23
|[490][Official HTML5 logo]
|~ L490:0-26
|Logo of HTML5
|January 2008
|    [491]HTML5 was published as a [492]Working Draft by the W3C.[493][49]
|~ L491:4-14 L492:34-52 L493:64-73
|    Although its syntax closely resembles that of [494]SGML, [495]HTML5 has
|~ L494:50-59 L495:61-71
|    abandoned any attempt to be an SGML application and has explicitly defined
|    its own "html" serialization, in addition to an alternative XML-based XHTML5
|    serialization.[496][50]
|~ L496:18-27
|2011 HTML5 - Last Call
|    On 14 February 2011, the W3C extended the charter of its HTML Working Group
|    with clear milestones for HTML5. In May 2011, the working group advanced
|    HTML5 to "Last Call", an invitation to communities inside and outside W3C to
|    confirm the technical soundness of the specification. The W3C developed a
|    comprehensive test suite to achieve broad interoperability for the full
|    specification by 2014, which was the target date for
|    recommendation.[497][51] In January 2011, the WHATWG renamed its "HTML5"
|~ L497:19-28
|    living standard to "HTML". The W3C nevertheless continued its project to
|    release HTML5.[498][52]
|~ L498:18-27
|2012 HTML5 - Candidate Recommendation
|    In July 2012, WHATWG and [499]W3C decided on a degree of separation. W3C
|~ L499:29-37
|    will continue the HTML5 specification work, focusing on a single definitive
|    standard, which is considered a "snapshot" by WHATWG. The WHATWG
|    organization will continue its work with HTML5 as a "Living Standard". The
|    concept of a living standard is that it is never complete and is always
|    being updated and improved. New features can be added but functionality will
|    not be removed.[500][53]
|~ L500:19-28
|    In December 2012, W3C designated HTML5 as a Candidate
|    Recommendation.[501][54] The criterion for advancement to [502]W3C
|~ L501:19-28 L502:62-70
|    Recommendation is "two 100% complete and fully interoperable
|~ L502:4-18
|    implementations".[503][55]
|~ L503:21-30
|2014 HTML5 - Proposed Recommendation and Recommendation
|    In September 2014, W3C moved HTML5 to Proposed Recommendation.[504][56]
|~ L504:66-75
|    On 28 October 2014, HTML5 was released as a stable W3C
|    Recommendation,[505][57] meaning the specification process is
|~ L505:19-28
|    complete.[506][58]
|~ L506:13-22
|
|XHTML versions
|~ B:0-14
|
|Main article: [507]XHTML
|~ L507:14-24
|
|XHTML is a separate language that began as a reformulation of HTML 4.01 using
|[508]XML 1.0. It is now referred to as the XML syntax for HTML and is no longer
|~ L508:0-8 U:39-62
|being developed as a separate standard.[509][59]
|~ L509:39-48
|
|  * XHTML 1.0 was published as a W3C Recommendation on January 26,
|    2000,[510][60] and was later revised and republished on August 1, 2002. It
|~ L510:9-18
|    offers the same three variations as HTML 4.0 and 4.01, reformulated in XML,
|    with minor restrictions.
|  * XHTML 1.1[511][61] was published as a W3C Recommendation on May 31, 2001. It
|~ L511:13-22
|    is based on XHTML 1.0 Strict, but includes minor changes, can be customized,
|    and is reformulated using modules in the W3C recommendation "Modularization
|    of XHTML", which was published on April 10, 2001.[512][62]
|~ L512:53-62
|  * XHTML 2.0 was a working draft. Work on it was abandoned in 2009 in favor of
|    work on [513]HTML5 and [514]XHTML5.[515][63][516][64][517][65] XHTML 2.0 was
|~ L513:12-22 L514:27-38 L515:39-48 L516:48-57 L517:57-66
|    incompatible with XHTML 1.x and, therefore, would be more accurately
|    characterized as an XHTML-inspired new language than an update to XHTML 1.x.
|
|Transition of HTML publication to WHATWG
|~ B:0-40
|
|See also: [518]HTML5 § W3C and WHATWG conflict
|~ L518:10-46
|
|On 28 May 2019, the W3C announced that WHATWG would be the sole publisher of the
|HTML and DOM standards.[519][66][520][67][521][68][522][69] The W3C and WHATWG
|~ L519:23-32 L520:32-41 L521:41-50 L522:50-59
|had been publishing competing standards since 2012. While the W3C standard was
|identical to the WHATWG in 2007 the standards have since progressively diverged
|due to different design decisions.[523][70] The WHATWG "Living Standard" had
|~ L523:34-43
|been the de facto web standard for some time.[524][71] The W3C periodically
|~ U:9-17 L524:45-54
|reviews and publishes snapshots of the WHATWG HTML specification as W3C
|Recommendations.[525][72]
|~ L525:16-25
|
|Markup
|~ B:0-6
|
|HTML markup consists of several key components, including those called tags (and
|~ U:71-75
|their attributes), character-based data types, character references and entity
|~ U:6-16 U:35-45 U:47-67 U:72-78
|references. HTML tags most commonly come in pairs like <h1> and </h1>, although
|~ U:0-10
|some represent empty elements and so are unpaired, for example <img>. The first
|~ U:15-29
|tag in such a pair is the start tag, and the second is the end tag (they are
|~ U:26-35 U:59-66
|also called opening tags and closing tags).
|~ U:12-24 U:29-41
|
|Another important component is the HTML [526]document type declaration, which
|~ UL526:40-70
|triggers [527]standards mode rendering.
|~ L527:9-28
|
|The following is an example of the classic [528]"Hello, World!" program:
|~ L528:43-71
|
|<!DOCTYPE html>
|<html>
|  <head>
|    <title>This is a title</title>
|  </head>
|  <body>
|    <div>
|        <p>Hello world!</p>
|    </div>
|  </body>
|</html>
|
|The text between <html> and </html> describes the web page, and the text between
|<body> and </body> is the visible page content. The markup text <title>This is a
|title</title> defines the browser page title shown on [529]browser tabs and
|~ L529:54-71
|[530]window titles and the tag <div> defines a division of the page used for
|~ L530:0-11
|easy styling. Between <head> and </head>, a <meta> element can be used to define
|webpage metadata.
|
|The Document Type Declaration <!DOCTYPE html> is for HTML5. If a declaration is
|not included, various browsers will revert to "[531]quirks mode" for
|~ L531:47-63
|rendering.[532][73]
|~ L532:10-19
|
|Elements
|~ B:0-8
|
|Main article: [533]HTML element
|~ L533:14-31
|[534][image]
|~ L534:0-12
|HTML element content categories
|
|HTML documents imply a structure of nested [535]HTML elements. These are
|~ L535:43-61
|indicated in the document by HTML tags, enclosed in angle
|~ U:34-38
|brackets.[536][74][[537]better source needed]
|~ L536:9-18 UL537:19-44
|
|In the simple, general case, the extent of an element is indicated by a pair of
|tags: a "start tag" <p> and "end tag" </p>. The text content of the element, if
|any, is placed between these tags.
|
|Tags may also enclose further tag markup between the start and end, including a
|mixture of tags and text. This indicates further (nested) elements, as children
|of the parent element.
|
|The start tag may also include the element's attributes within the tag. These
|~ U:45-55
|indicate other information, such as identifiers for sections within the
|document, identifiers used to bind style information to the presentation of the
|document, and for some tags such as the <img> used to embed images, the
|reference to the image resource in the format like this: <img
|src="example.com/example.jpg">
|
|Some elements, such as the [538]line break <br> do not permit any embedded
|~ L538:27-42 U:62-65
|content, either text or further tags. These require only a single empty tag
|(akin to a start tag) and do not use an end tag.
|
|Many tags, particularly the closing end tag for the very commonly used paragraph
|element <p>, are optional. An HTML browser or other agent can infer the closure
|for the end of an element from the context and the structural rules defined by
|the HTML standard. These rules are complex and not widely understood by most
|HTML authors.
|
|The general form of an HTML element is therefore: <tag attribute1="value1"
|attribute2="value2">''content''</tag>. Some HTML elements are defined as empty
|~ U:73-78
|elements and take the form <tag attribute1="value1" attribute2="value2">. Empty
|~ U:0-8
|elements may enclose no content, for instance, the <br> tag or the inline <img>
|tag. The name of an HTML element is the name used in the tags. The end tag's
|name is preceded by a slash character /. If a tag has no content, an end tag is
|not allowed. If attributes are not mentioned, default values are used in each
|case.
|
|Element examples
|~ B:0-16
|
|See also: [539]HTML element
|~ L539:10-27
|
|Header of the HTML document: <head>...</head>. The title is included in the
|head, for example:
|
|<head>
|  <title>The Title</title>
|  <link rel="stylesheet" href="stylebyjimbowales.css"> <!-- Imports Stylesheets 
|-->
|</head>
|
|Headings
|~ B:0-8
|
|HTML headings are defined with the <h1> to <h6> tags with H1 being the highest
|(or most important) level and H6 the least:
|
|<h1>Heading level 1</h1>
|<h2>Heading level 2</h2>
|<h3>Heading level 3</h3>
|<h4>Heading level 4</h4>
|<h5>Heading level 5</h5>
|<h6>Heading level 6</h6>
|
|The effects are:
|
|    Heading Level 1
|    Heading Level 2
|    Heading Level 3
|    Heading Level 4
|    Heading Level 5
|    Heading Level 6
|
|CSS can substantially change the rendering.
|
|Paragraphs:
|
|<p>Paragraph 1</p> <p>Paragraph 2</p>
|
|Line breaks
|~ B:0-11
|
|<br>. The difference between <br> and <p> is that <br> [540]breaks a line
|~ L540:55-73
|without altering the semantic structure of the page, whereas <p> sections the
|page into [541]paragraphs. The element <br> is an empty element in that,
|~ L541:10-25 U:50-63
|although it may have attributes, it can take no content and it must not have an
|end tag.
|
|<p>This <br> is a paragraph <br> with <br> line breaks</p>
|
|Links
|~ B:0-5
|
|This is a link in HTML. To create a link the <a> tag is used. The href attribute
|holds the [542]URL address of the link.
|~ L542:10-18
|
|<a href="https://www.wikipedia.org/">A link to Wikipedia!</a>
|
|Inputs
|~ B:0-6
|
|There are many possible ways a user can give inputs like:
|
|<input type="text"> <!-- This is for text input -->
|<input type="file"> <!-- This is for uploading files -->
|<input type="checkbox"> <!-- This is for checkboxes -->
|
|Comments
|~ B:0-8
|
|<!-- This is a comment -->
|
|Comments can help in the understanding of the markup and do not display in the
|webpage.
|
|There are several types of markup elements used in HTML:
|
|  * Structural markup indicates the purpose of text:
|      * For example, <h2>Golf</h2> establishes "Golf" as a second-level
|        [543]heading. Structural markup does not denote any specific rendering,
|~ L543:8-20
|        but most web browsers have default styles for element formatting.
|        Content may be further styled using [544]Cascading Style Sheets
|~ L544:44-71
|        (CSS).[545][75]
|~ L545:14-23
|  * Presentational markup indicates the appearance of the text, regardless of
|    its purpose:
|      * For example, <b>bold text</b> indicates that visual output devices
|        should render "boldface" in bold text, but gives little indication what
|        devices that are unable to do this (such as aural devices that read the
|        text aloud) should do. In the case of both <b>bold text</b> and
|        <i>italic text</i>, there are other elements that may have equivalent
|        visual renderings but that are more semantic in nature, such as
|        <strong>strong text</strong> and <em>emphasized text</em> respectively.
|        It is easier to see how an aural user agent should interpret the latter
|        two elements. However, they are not equivalent to their presentational
|        counterparts: it would be undesirable for a screen reader to emphasize
|        the name of a book, for instance, but on a screen, such a name would be
|        italicized. Most presentational markup elements have become
|        [546]deprecated under the HTML 4.0 specification in favor of using
|~ L546:8-23
|        [547]CSS for styling.
|~ L547:8-16
|  * Hypertext markup makes parts of a document into links to other documents:
|      * An anchor element creates a [548]hyperlink in the document and its href
|~ L548:36-50
|        attribute sets the link's target [549]URL. For example, the HTML markup
|~ L549:41-49
|        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/">Wikipedia</a>, will render the word
|        "[550]Wikipedia" as a hyperlink. To render an image as a hyperlink, an
|~ L550:9-23
|        img element is inserted as content into the a element. Like br, img is
|        an empty element with attributes but no content or closing tag. <a
|        href="https://example.org"><img src="image.gif" alt="alternative text"
|        width="50" height="50" border="0"></a>.
|
|Attributes
|~ B:0-10
|
|Main article: [551]HTML attribute
|~ L551:14-33
|
|Most of the attributes of an element are [552]name-value pairs, separated by =
|~ L552:41-62
|and written within the start tag of an element after the element's name. The
|value may be enclosed in single or double quotes, although values consisting of
|certain characters can be left unquoted in HTML (but not
|XHTML).[553][76][554][77] Leaving attribute values unquoted is considered
|~ L553:7-16 L554:16-25
|unsafe.[555][78] In contrast with name-value pair attributes, there are some
|~ L555:7-16
|attributes that affect the element simply by their presence in the start tag of
|the element,[556][7] like the ismap attribute for the img element.[557][79]
|~ L556:12-20 L557:66-75
|
|There are several common attributes that may appear in many elements:
|
|  * The id attribute provides a document-wide unique identifier for an element.
|    This is used to identify the element so that stylesheets can alter its
|    presentational properties, and scripts may alter, animate or delete its
|    contents or presentation. Appended to the URL of the page, it provides a
|    globally unique identifier for the element, typically a sub-section of the
|    page. For example, the ID "Attributes" in
|    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTML#Attributes.
|  * The class attribute provides a way of classifying similar elements. This can
|    be used for [558]semantic or presentation purposes. For example, an HTML
|~ L558:16-29
|    document might semantically use the designation <class="notation"> to
|    indicate that all elements with this class value are subordinate to the main
|    text of the document. In presentation, such elements might be gathered
|    together and presented as footnotes on a page instead of appearing in the
|    place where they occur in the HTML source. Class attributes are used
|    semantically in [559]microformats. Multiple class values may be specified;
|~ L559:20-37
|    for example <class="notation important"> puts the element into both the
|    notation and the important classes.
|  * An author may use the style attribute to assign presentational properties to
|    a particular element. It is considered better practice to use an element's
|    id or class attributes to select the element from within a [560]stylesheet,
|~ L560:63-78
|    though sometimes this can be too cumbersome for a simple, specific, or ad
|    hoc styling.
|  * The title attribute is used to attach a subtextual explanation to an
|    element. In most [561]browsers this attribute is displayed as a
|~ L561:21-34
|    [562]tooltip.
|~ L562:4-16
|  * The lang attribute identifies the natural language of the element's
|    contents, which may be different from that of the rest of the document. For
|    example, in an English-language document:
|
|    <p>Oh well, <span lang="fr">c'est la vie</span>, as they say in France.</p>
|
|The abbreviation element, abbr, can be used to demonstrate some of these
|attributes:
|
|<abbr id="anId" class="jargon" style="color:purple;" title="Hypertext Markup Lan
|guage">HTML</abbr>
|
|This example displays as HTML; in most browsers, pointing the cursor at the
|abbreviation should display the title text "Hypertext Markup Language."
|
|Most elements take the language-related attribute dir to specify text direction,
|such as with "rtl" for right-to-left text in, for example, [563]Arabic,
|~ L563:59-70
|[564]Persian or [565]Hebrew.[566][80]
|~ L564:0-12 L565:16-27 L566:28-37
|
|Character and entity references
|~ B:0-31
|
|See also: [567]List of XML and HTML character entity references and [568]Unicode
|~ L567:10-63 L568:68-80
|and HTML
|~ L568:0-8
|
|As of version 4.0, HTML defines a set of 252 [569]character entity references
|~ L569:45-77
|and a set of 1,114,050 [570]numeric character references, both of which allow
|~ L570:23-56
|individual characters to be written via simple markup, rather than literally. A
|literal character and its markup counterpart are considered equivalent and are
|rendered identically.
|
|The ability to "[571]escape" characters in this way allows for the characters <
|~ L571:16-27
|and & (when written as &lt; and &amp;, respectively) to be interpreted as
|character data, rather than markup. For example, a literal < normally indicates
|the start of a tag, and & normally indicates the start of a character entity
|reference or numeric character reference; writing it as &amp; or &#x26; or &#38;
|allows & to be included in the content of an element or in the value of an
|attribute. The double-quote character ("), when not used to quote an attribute
|value, must also be escaped as &quot; or &#x22; or &#34; when it appears within
|the attribute value itself. Equivalently, the single-quote character ('), when
|not used to quote an attribute value, must also be escaped as &#x27; or &#39;
|(or as &apos; in HTML5 or XHTML documents[572][81][573][82]) when it appears
|~ L572:41-50 L573:50-59
|within the attribute value itself. If document authors overlook the need to
|escape such characters, some browsers can be very forgiving and try to use
|context to guess their intent. The result is still invalid markup, which makes
|the document less accessible to other browsers and to other [574]user agents
|~ L574:60-76
|that may try to parse the document for [575]search and indexing purposes for
|~ L575:39-63
|example.
|
|Escaping also allows for characters that are not easily typed, or that are not
|available in the document's [576]character encoding, to be represented within
|~ L576:28-51
|the element and attribute content. For example, the acute-accented e (é), a
|character typically found only on Western European and South American keyboards,
|can be written in any HTML document as the entity reference &eacute; or as the
|numeric references &#xE9; or &#233;, using characters that are available on all
|keyboards and are supported in all character encodings. [577]Unicode character
|~ L577:56-68
|encodings such as [578]UTF-8 are compatible with all modern browsers and allow
|~ L578:18-28
|direct access to almost all the characters of the world's writing
|systems.[579][83]
|~ L579:8-17
|
|HTML escape sequence examples
|Named   Decimal   Hexadecimal   Result   Description   Notes
|&amp;   &#38;   &#x26;   &   [580]Ampersand
|~ L580:29-43
|&lt;   &#60;   &#x3C;   <   [581]Less Than
|~ L581:28-42
|&gt;   &#62;   &#x3E;   >   [582]Greater Than
|~ L582:28-45
|&quot;   &#34;   &#x22;   "   [583]Double Quote
|~ L583:30-47
|&apos;   &#39;   &#x27;   '   [584]Single Quote
|~ L584:30-47
|&nbsp;   &#160;   &#xA0;       [585]Non-Breaking Space
|~ L585:31-54
|&copy;   &#169;   &#xA9;   ©   [586]Copyright
|~ L586:31-45
|&reg;   &#174;   &#xAE;   ®   [587]Registered Trademark
|~ L587:30-55
|&dagger;   &#8224;   &#x2020;   ?   [588]Dagger
|~ L588:36-47
|&ddagger;   &#8225;   &#x2021;   ?   [589]Double dagger   Names are
|~ L589:37-55
|case-sensitive and may have synonyms.
|&trade;   &#8482;   &#x2122;   (tm)   [590]Trademark
|~ L590:38-52
|
|Data types
|~ B:0-10
|
|HTML defines several [591]data types for element content, such as script data
|~ L591:21-36
|and stylesheet data, and a plethora of types for attribute values, including
|IDs, names, [592]URIs, numbers, units of length, languages, media descriptors,
|~ L592:12-21
|colors, character encodings, dates and times, and so on. All of these data types
|are specializations of character data.
|
|Document type declaration
|~ B:0-25
|
|HTML documents are required to start with a [593]document type declaration
|~ L593:44-74
|(informally, a "doctype"). In browsers, the doctype helps to define the
|rendering mode-particularly whether to use [594]quirks mode.
|~ L594:43-59
|
|The original purpose of the doctype was to enable the parsing and validation of
|HTML documents by SGML tools based on the [595]document type definition (DTD).
|~ L595:42-71
|The DTD to which the DOCTYPE refers contains a machine-readable grammar
|specifying the permitted and prohibited content for a document conforming to
|such a DTD. Browsers, on the other hand, do not implement HTML as an application
|of SGML and as consequence do not read the DTD.
|
|[596]HTML5 does not define a DTD; therefore, in HTML5 the doctype declaration is
|~ L596:0-10
|simpler and shorter:[597][84]
|~ L597:20-29
|
|<!DOCTYPE html>
|
|An example of an HTML 4 doctype
|
|<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN" "https://www.w3.org/TR/html4/s
|trict.dtd">
|
|This declaration references the DTD for the "strict" version of HTML 4.01.
|SGML-based validators read the DTD in order to properly parse the document and
|to perform validation. In modern browsers, a valid doctype activates standards
|mode as opposed to [598]quirks mode.
|~ L598:19-35
|
|In addition, HTML 4.01 provides Transitional and Frameset DTDs, [599]as
|~ L599:64-71
|explained below. The transitional type is the most inclusive, incorporating
|~ L599:0-15
|current tags as well as older or "deprecated" tags, with the Strict DTD
|excluding deprecated tags. The frameset has all tags necessary to make frames on
|a page along with the tags included in transitional type.[600][85]
|~ L600:57-66
|
|Semantic HTML
|~ B:0-13
|
|Main article: [601]Semantic HTML
|~ L601:14-32
|
|Semantic HTML is a way of writing HTML that emphasizes the meaning of the
|encoded information over its presentation (look). HTML has included semantic
|markup from its inception,[602][86] but has also included presentational markup,
|~ L602:26-35
|such as <font>, <i>, and <center> tags. There are also the semantically neutral
|[603]div and span tags. Since the late 1990s, when [604]Cascading Style Sheets
|~ L603:0-17 L604:51-78
|were beginning to work in most browsers, web authors have been encouraged to
|avoid the use of presentational HTML markup with a view to the [605]separation
|~ L605:63-78
|of content and presentation.[606][87]
|~ L605:0-27 L606:28-37
|
|In a 2001 discussion of the [607]Semantic Web, [608]Tim Berners-Lee and others
|~ L607:28-45 L608:47-67
|gave examples of ways in which intelligent software "agents" may one day
|automatically crawl the web and find, filter, and correlate previously
|unrelated, published facts for the benefit of human users.[609][88] Such agents
|~ L609:58-67
|are not commonplace even now[[610]when?], but some of the ideas of [611]Web 2.0,
|~ UL610:29-39 L611:67-79
|[612]mashups and [613]price comparison websites may be coming
|~ L612:0-12 L613:17-47
|close[[614]citation needed]. The main difference between these web application
|~ UL614:6-26
|hybrids and Berners-Lee's semantic agents lies in the fact that the current
|[615]aggregation and hybridization of information is usually designed by
|~ L615:0-16
|[616]web developers, who already know the web locations and the [617]API
|~ L616:0-19 L617:64-72
|semantics of the specific data they wish to mash, compare and combine.
|~ L617:0-9
|
|An important type of web agent that does crawl and read web pages automatically,
|without prior knowledge of what it might find, is the [618]web crawler or
|~ L618:54-70
|search-engine spider. These software agents are dependent on the semantic
|clarity of web pages they find as they use various techniques and
|[619]algorithms to read and index millions of web pages a day and provide web
|~ L619:0-15
|users with [620]search facilities without which the World Wide Web's usefulness
|~ L620:11-33
|would be greatly reduced.
|
|In order for search engine spiders to be able to rate the significance of pieces
|of text they find in HTML documents, and also for those creating mashups and
|other hybrids as well as for more automated agents as they are developed, the
|semantic structures that exist in HTML need to be widely and uniformly applied
|to bring out the meaning of the published text.[621][89]
|~ L621:47-56
|
|Presentational markup tags are [622]deprecated in current HTML and [623]XHTML
|~ L622:31-46 L623:67-77
|recommendations. The majority of presentational features from previous versions
|of HTML are no longer allowed as they lead to poorer accessibility, higher cost
|of site maintenance, and larger document sizes.[624][90]
|~ L624:47-56
|
|Good semantic HTML also improves the [625]accessibility of web documents (see
|~ L625:37-55
|also [626]Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). For example, when a screen
|~ L626:5-46
|reader or audio browser can correctly ascertain the structure of a document, it
|will not waste the visually impaired user's time by reading out repeated or
|irrelevant information when it has been marked up correctly.
|
|Delivery
|~ B:0-8
|
|HTML documents can be delivered by the same means as any other computer file.
|However, they are most often delivered either by [627]HTTP from a [628]web
|~ L627:49-58 L628:66-74
|server or by [629]email.
|~ L628:0-6 L629:13-23
|
|HTTP
|~ B:0-4
|
|Main article: [630]HTTP
|~ L630:14-23
|
|The [631]World Wide Web is composed primarily of HTML documents transmitted from
|~ L631:4-23
|web servers to web browsers using the [632]Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).
|~ L632:38-70
|However, HTTP is used to serve images, sound, and other content, in addition to
|HTML. To allow the web browser to know how to handle each document it receives,
|other information is transmitted along with the document. This [633]meta data
|~ L633:63-77
|usually includes the [634]MIME type (e.g., text/html or application/xhtml+xml)
|~ L634:21-35
|and the character encoding (see [635]Character encodings in HTML).
|~ L635:32-64
|
|In modern browsers, the MIME type that is sent with the HTML document may affect
|how the document is initially interpreted. A document sent with the XHTML MIME
|type is expected to be [636]well-formed XML; syntax errors may cause the browser
|~ L636:23-39
|to fail to render it. The same document sent with the HTML MIME type might be
|displayed successfully since some browsers are more lenient with HTML.
|
|The W3C recommendations state that XHTML 1.0 documents that follow guidelines
|set forth in the recommendation's Appendix C may be labeled with either MIME
|Type.[637][91] XHTML 1.1 also states that XHTML 1.1 documents should[638][92] be
|~ L637:5-14 L638:68-77
|labeled with either MIME type.[639][93]
|~ L639:30-39
|
|HTML e-mail
|~ B:0-11
|
|Main article: [640]HTML email
|~ L640:14-29
|
|Most graphical email clients allow the use of a subset of HTML (often
|ill-defined) to provide formatting and [641]semantic markup not available with
|~ L641:39-52
|[642]plain text. This may include typographic information like colored headings,
|~ L642:0-15
|emphasized and quoted text, inline images and diagrams. Many such clients
|include both a [643]GUI editor for composing HTML e-mail messages and a
|~ L643:15-23
|rendering engine for displaying them. Use of HTML in e-mail is criticized by
|some because of compatibility issues, because it can help disguise [644]phishing
|~ L644:67-80
|attacks, because of accessibility issues for blind or visually impaired people,
|because it can confuse [645]spam filters and because the message size is larger
|~ L645:23-32
|than plain text.
|
|Naming conventions
|~ B:0-18
|
|The most common [646]filename extension for [647]files containing HTML is .html.
|~ L646:16-39 L647:44-54
|A common abbreviation of this is .htm, which originated because some operating
|systems limit file extensions to [648]three letters.[649][94]
|~ L648:33-51 L649:52-61
|
|HTML Application
|~ B:0-16
|
|Main article: [650]HTML Application
|~ L650:14-35
|
|An HTML Application (HTA; file extension .hta) is a [651]Microsoft Windows
|~ L651:52-74
|application that uses HTML and Dynamic HTML in a [652]browser to provide the
|~ L652:49-61
|application's graphical interface. A regular HTML file is confined to the
|security model of the [653]web browser's security, communicating only to web
|~ L653:22-49
|servers and manipulating only web page objects and [654]site cookies. An HTA
|~ L654:51-68
|runs as a fully trusted application and therefore has more privileges, like
|creation/editing/removal of files and [655]Windows Registry entries. Because
|~ L655:38-59
|they operate outside the browser's security model, HTAs cannot be executed via
|HTTP, but must be downloaded (just like an [656]EXE file) and executed from
|~ L656:43-51
|local file system.
|
|HTML4 variations
|~ B:0-16
|
|Since its inception, HTML and its associated protocols gained acceptance
|relatively quickly. However, no clear standards existed in the early years of
|the language. Though its creators originally conceived of HTML as a semantic
|language devoid of presentation details,[657][95] practical uses pushed many
|~ L657:40-49
|presentational elements and attributes into the language, driven largely by the
|various browser vendors. The latest standards surrounding HTML reflect efforts
|to overcome the sometimes chaotic development of the language[658][96] and to
|~ L658:61-70
|create a rational foundation for building both meaningful and well-presented
|documents. To return HTML to its role as a semantic language, the [659]W3C has
|~ L659:66-74
|developed style languages such as [660]CSS and [661]XSL to shoulder the burden
|~ L660:34-42 L661:47-55
|of presentation. In conjunction, the HTML specification has slowly reined in the
|presentational elements.
|
|There are two axes differentiating various variations of HTML as currently
|specified: SGML-based HTML versus XML-based HTML (referred to as XHTML) on one
|axis, and strict versus transitional (loose) versus frameset on the other axis.
|
|SGML-based versus XML-based HTML
|~ B:0-32
|
|One difference in the latest[[662]when?] HTML specifications lies in the
|~ UL662:29-39
|distinction between the SGML-based specification and the XML-based
|specification. The XML-based specification is usually called [663]XHTML to
|~ L663:61-71
|distinguish it clearly from the more traditional definition. However, the root
|element name continues to be "html" even in the XHTML-specified HTML. The W3C
|intended XHTML 1.0 to be identical to HTML 4.01 except where limitations of XML
|over the more complex SGML require workarounds. Because XHTML and HTML are
|closely related, they are sometimes documented in parallel. In such
|circumstances, some authors [664]conflate the two names as (X)HTML or X(HTML).
|~ L664:28-55
|
|Like HTML 4.01, XHTML 1.0 has three sub-specifications: strict, transitional,
|and frameset.
|
|Aside from the different opening declarations for a document, the differences
|between an HTML 4.01 and XHTML 1.0 document-in each of the corresponding
|DTDs-are largely syntactic. The underlying syntax of HTML allows many shortcuts
|that XHTML does not, such as elements with optional opening or closing tags, and
|even empty elements which must not have an end tag. By contrast, XHTML requires
|all elements to have an opening tag and a closing tag. XHTML, however, also
|introduces a new shortcut: an XHTML tag may be opened and closed within the same
|tag, by including a slash before the end of the tag like this: <br/>. The
|introduction of this shorthand, which is not used in the SGML declaration for
|HTML 4.01, may confuse earlier software unfamiliar with this new convention. A
|fix for this is remove the slash preceding the closing angle bracket, as such:
|<br>.[665][97]
|~ L665:5-14
|
|To understand the subtle differences between HTML and XHTML, consider the
|transformation of a valid and well-formed XHTML 1.0 document that adheres to
|Appendix C (see below) into a valid HTML 4.01 document. Making this translation
|requires the following steps:
|
|  1. The language for an element is specified with a lang attribute rather than
|     the XHTML xml:lang attribute. XHTML uses XML's built-in language-defining
|     functionality attribute.
|  2. Remove the XML namespace (xmlns=URI). HTML has no facilities for
|     namespaces.
|  3. Change the document type declaration from XHTML 1.0 to HTML 4.01.
|  4. If present, remove the XML declaration. Typically this is: <?xml
|     version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>.
|  5. Ensure that the document's MIME type is set to text/html. For both HTML and
|     XHTML, this comes from the HTTP Content-Type header sent by the server.
|  6. Change the XML empty-element syntax to an HTML style empty element (<br/>
|     to <br>).
|
|Those are the main changes necessary to translate a document from XHTML 1.0 to
|HTML 4.01. To translate from HTML to XHTML would also require the addition of
|any omitted opening or closing tags. Whether coding in HTML or XHTML it may just
|be best to always include the optional tags within an HTML document rather than
|remembering which tags can be omitted.
|
|A well-formed XHTML document adheres to all the syntax requirements of XML. A
|valid document adheres to the content specification for XHTML, which describes
|the document structure.
|
|The W3C recommends several conventions to ensure an easy migration between HTML
|and XHTML.[666][98] The following steps can be applied to XHTML 1.0 documents
|~ L666:10-19
|only:
|
|  * Include both xml:lang and lang attributes on any elements assigning
|    language.
|  * Use the empty-element syntax only for elements specified as empty in HTML.
|  * Remove the closing slash in empty-element tags: for example <br> instead of
|    <br/>.
|  * Include explicit close tags for elements that permit content but are left
|    empty (for example, , not <div />).
|  * Omit the XML declaration.
|
|By carefully following the W3C's compatibility guidelines, a user agent should
|be able to interpret the document equally as HTML or XHTML. For documents that
|are XHTML 1.0 and have been made compatible in this way, the W3C permits them to
|be served either as HTML (with a text/html [667]MIME type), or as XHTML (with an
|~ L667:43-57
|application/xhtml+xml or application/xml MIME type). When delivered as XHTML,
|browsers should use an XML parser, which adheres strictly to the XML
|specifications for parsing the document's contents.
|
|Transitional versus strict
|~ B:0-26
|
|HTML 4 defined three different versions of the language: Strict, Transitional
|(once called Loose), and Frameset. The Strict version is intended for new
|documents and is considered best practice, while the Transitional and Frameset
|versions were developed to make it easier to transition documents that conformed
|to older HTML specifications or did not conform to any specification to a
|version of HTML 4. The Transitional and Frameset versions allow for
|presentational markup, which is omitted in the Strict version. Instead,
|[668]cascading style sheets are encouraged to improve the presentation of HTML
|~ L668:0-27
|documents. Because XHTML 1 only defines an XML syntax for the language defined
|by HTML 4, the same differences apply to XHTML 1 as well.
|
|The Transitional version allows the following parts of the vocabulary, which are
|not included in the Strict version:
|
|  * A looser content model
|      * Inline elements and plain text are allowed directly in: body,
|        blockquote, form, noscript and noframes
|  * Presentation related elements
|      * underline (u) (Deprecated. can confuse a visitor with a hyperlink.)
|      * strike-through (s)
|      * center (Deprecated. use CSS instead.)
|      * font (Deprecated. use CSS instead.)
|      * basefont (Deprecated. use CSS instead.)
|  * Presentation related attributes
|      * background (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) and bgcolor (Deprecated. use
|        CSS instead.) attributes for body (required element according to the
|        W3C.) element.
|      * align (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) attribute on div, form, paragraph
|        (p) and heading (h1...h6) elements
|      * align (Deprecated. use CSS instead.), noshade (Deprecated. use CSS
|        instead.), size (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) and width (Deprecated.
|        use CSS instead.) attributes on hr element
|      * align (Deprecated. use CSS instead.), border, vspace and hspace
|        attributes on img and object (caution: the object element is only
|        supported in Internet Explorer (from the major browsers)) elements
|      * align (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) attribute on legend and caption
|        elements
|      * align (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) and bgcolor (Deprecated. use CSS
|        instead.) on table element
|      * nowrap (Obsolete), bgcolor (Deprecated. use CSS instead.), width, height
|        on td and th elements
|      * bgcolor (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) attribute on tr element
|      * clear (Obsolete) attribute on br element
|      * compact attribute on dl, dir and menu elements
|      * type (Deprecated. use CSS instead.), compact (Deprecated. use CSS
|        instead.) and start (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) attributes on ol and
|        ul elements
|      * type and value attributes on li element
|      * width attribute on pre element
|  * Additional elements in Transitional specification
|      * menu (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) list (no substitute, though the
|        unordered list, is recommended)
|      * dir (Deprecated. use CSS instead.) list (no substitute, though the
|        unordered list is recommended)
|      * isindex (Deprecated.) (element requires server-side support and is
|        typically added to documents server-side, form and input elements can be
|        used as a substitute)
|      * applet (Deprecated. use the object element instead.)
|  * The language (Obsolete) attribute on script element (redundant with the type
|    attribute).
|  * Frame related entities
|      * iframe
|      * noframes
|      * target (Deprecated in the map, link and form elements.) attribute on a,
|        client-side image-map (map), link, form and base elements
|
|The Frameset version includes everything in the Transitional version, as well as
|the frameset element (used instead of body) and the frame element.
|
|Frameset versus transitional
|~ B:0-28
|
|In addition to the above transitional differences, the frameset specifications
|(whether XHTML 1.0 or HTML 4.01) specify a different content model, with
|frameset replacing body, that contains either frame elements, or optionally
|noframes with a body.
|
|Summary of specification versions
|~ B:0-33
|
|As this list demonstrates, the loose versions of the specification are
|maintained for legacy support. However, contrary to popular misconceptions, the
|move to XHTML does not imply a removal of this legacy support. Rather the X in
|XML stands for extensible and the W3C is modularizing the entire specification
|and opens it up to independent extensions. The primary achievement in the move
|from XHTML 1.0 to XHTML 1.1 is the modularization of the entire specification.
|The strict version of HTML is deployed in XHTML 1.1 through a set of modular
|extensions to the base XHTML 1.1 specification. Likewise, someone looking for
|the loose (transitional) or frameset specifications will find similar extended
|XHTML 1.1 support (much of it is contained in the legacy or frame modules).
|Modularization also allows for separate features to develop on their own
|timetable. So for example, XHTML 1.1 will allow quicker migration to emerging
|XML standards such as [669]MathML (a presentational and semantic math language
|~ L669:22-33
|based on XML) and [670]XForms-a new highly advanced web-form technology to
|~ L670:18-29
|replace the existing HTML forms.
|
|In summary, the HTML 4 specification primarily reined in all the various HTML
|implementations into a single clearly written specification based on SGML. XHTML
|1.0, ported this specification, as is, to the new XML-defined specification.
|Next, XHTML 1.1 takes advantage of the extensible nature of XML and modularizes
|the whole specification. XHTML 2.0 was intended to be the first step in adding
|new features to the specification in a standards-body-based
|approach.[[671]AI-generated?]
|~ UL671:10-28
|
|WHATWG HTML versus HTML5
|~ B:0-24
|
|Main article: [672]HTML § Transition of HTML publication to WHATWG
|~ L672:14-66
|
|The HTML Living Standard, which is developed by WHATWG, is the official version,
|while W3C HTML5 is no longer separate from WHATWG.
|
|WYSIWYG editors
|~ B:0-15
|
|[673][icon]
|~ L673:0-11
|This article is missing information about contenteditable. Please expand the
|~ B:13-35
|article [674]by making an edit requestto include this information. Further
|~ L674:8-38
|details may exist on the [675]talk page. (January 2021)
|~ L675:25-39 U:41-55
|
|[676]WYSIWYG editors (what you see is what you get), are HTML editors in which
|~ L676:0-12 U:22-50
|the user lays out everything as it is to appear in the HTML document using a
|[677]graphical user interface (GUI), such as [678]Microsoft FrontPage, often
|~ L677:0-29 L678:45-69
|similar to [679]word processors. The editor renders the document rather than
|~ L679:11-31
|showing the code, so authors do not require extensive knowledge of HTML.
|
|The WYSIWYG editing model has been criticized,[680][99][681][100] primarily
|~ L680:46-55 L681:55-65
|because of the low quality of the generated code; there are various developers
|advocating a change to the [682]WYSIWYM model (what you see is what you
|~ L682:27-39 U:47-71
|mean).[683][101]
|~ U:0-4 L683:6-16
|
|WYSIWYG editors remain a controversial topic because of their perceived flaws
|such as:
|
|  * Relying mainly on the layout as opposed to meaning, often using markup that
|    does not convey the intended meaning but simply copies the layout.[684][102]
|~ L684:70-80
|  * Often producing extremely verbose and redundant code that fails to make use
|    of the cascading nature of HTML and [685]CSS.
|~ L685:40-48
|  * Often producing ungrammatical markup, called [686]tag soup or semantically
|~ L686:49-62
|    incorrect markup (such as <em> for italics).
|  * As a great deal of the information in HTML documents is not in the layout,
|    the model has been criticized for its "what you see is all you
|    get"-nature.[687][103]
|~ L687:16-26
|
